<h2>Common Insurer Arguments on Hail Claims</h2>
<p>Hail claims are often disputed on the details. Arguments that come up frequently include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>"Wear and tear, not hail."</strong> The insurer attributes granule loss or cracking to age, blistering, foot traffic, or manufacturing defects. On the coast, salt-air corrosion may be cited for metal components.</li>
<li><strong>"Prior storm."</strong> The carrier says the damage happened before the policy period or during an earlier, unreported storm. Nueces County can see more than one hail day in a season, which makes dating the damage important.</li>
<li><strong>"Cosmetic only."</strong> Dents in metal roofing or soft metals are labeled cosmetic under an exclusion endorsement.</li>
<li><strong>Repair vs. replace.</strong> The estimate allows spot repairs even when brittle or discontinued materials cannot be repaired or matched.</li>
<li><strong>Missing line items.</strong> Drip edge, starter course, ridge, flashing, and code-required items are left out. Our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> looks for these gaps.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your hail claim was denied, ask the insurer to explain the denial in writing and identify the policy language it relied on. Our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page explains common next steps.</p>

<h2>Settlement Factors: Deductibles, Depreciation, and Code</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind/hail deductibles.</strong> Many Texas policies, including many TWIA policies, use a separate wind/hail deductible, often a percentage of the dwelling limit. Check your declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>ACV vs. RCV.</strong> Under replacement cost coverage, insurers typically pay actual cash value first and release recoverable depreciation after repairs are completed. Some roof coverage is ACV-only or follows a payment schedule. See <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law.</strong> Corpus Christi adopted the 2021 International Residential Code with local amendments effective August 1, 2023, and re-roofing requires a city permit with a TDI WPI-1 form. Code-required upgrades are paid only under ordinance or law coverage, up to its limit.</li>
<li><strong>Limits.</strong> Other structures and personal property have their own limits.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Hail Damage Beyond Corpus Christi City Limits</h2>
<p>Hail swaths do not stop at a city line, and some of the largest stones in the NOAA record fell in smaller communities. Baseball-size hail broke a car windshield near Petronila in May 2006, and the May 1993 storm brought golf ball to tennis ball-size hail to Banquete, west of Robstown, where the Red Cross reported roof and window damage to homes. Neighboring counties see it too: NOAA lists five reports of baseball-size or larger hail in Kleberg County and 17 reports of golf ball-size or larger hail in San Patricio County. Whether your property is in Calallen, Robstown, Kingsville, Portland, or on the islands, the same documentation steps apply. See our <a href="/service-areas/">service areas</a> for the communities we serve.</p>

<h2>How a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster represents the policyholder, not the insurer. On a hail claim, that may include inspecting the property, reviewing the policy and endorsements, preparing a detailed estimate, gathering weather data, and communicating with the insurer's adjuster. If the claim was already paid low, we can review it for a supplement or discuss whether appraisal may fit a dispute over the amount of loss. We cannot promise a result; outcomes depend on your policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Give prompt notice</strong> of the loss. For TWIA, the claim must be filed within one year of the date of damage.</li>
<li><strong>Prevent further damage</strong> by tarping openings and boarding broken windows, and keep receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Preserve evidence</strong> by photographing damage and keeping samples of damaged materials when practical.</li>
<li><strong>Submit a proof of loss</strong> if the insurer requests one, within the time the policy requires.</li>
<li><strong>Cooperate</strong> with inspections and reasonable document requests.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many policies also set a deadline to file suit. For legal questions about deadlines or a disputed claim, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
